1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. g3 Bd7 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. O-O
Bg7 9. Nde2 Qc8 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. exd5 Ne5 12. Be3 O-O 13. Bd4 Bh3 14. b3 Bxg2
15. Kxg2 Qf5 16. f3 Rfc8 17. c3 h5 18. Qb1 Qd7 19. h3 Qb5 20. c4 Qb4 21. Bc3
Qc5 22. Bd4 Qb4 23. f4 Qd2 24. fxe5 dxe5 25. Qb2 Qd3 26. Rf3 Qe4 27. Be3 $2 {
Hard to believe both Alexander and Aitken missed a fairly simple tactic here
but Aitken's own recording of the game confirms it (see final note).} g5 (27...
Qxf3+ $1 28. Kxf3 e4+ {with a likely Black win. Dugald MacIsaac, in his
Glasgow Herald column of 12 Nov 1948, sticks to his original version of the
score when he points out the above trick, saying "this surprising error,
spotted by several of our readers, seems to have been generally overlooked."})
28. Qb1 {Source: Glasgow Herald, 22 Oct 1948 (a few moves of the game given in
BCM, Oct 1948, p345). Later note: "Aitken scorebook 9 confirms this was a
double blunder - he gives 27.Be3?? and 27...g5?? at the end of the game and
adds: '27.Be3 was a blunder and 27...Qxf3+ wins for black!' Geoff Chandler,
November 2020)} 1-0

