1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O d6 6. d3 b5 7. Bb3 Na5 8. c3
Nxb3 9. axb3 Be7 10. h3 Bd7 11. b4 Qc8 12. Re1 Qb7 13. Nbd2 g5 14. Nxg5 Rg8 15.
h4 c5 16. bxc5 dxc5 17. Qb3 Rg7 18. Ngf3 O-O-O $5 {Aitken} 19. Kf1 Rdg8 20. g3
Bh3+ 21. Ke2 Bg4 22. Qa2 $1 {Aitken} c4 23. Qxa6 cxd3+ 24. Kxd3 Nd7 25. Qxb7+
Kxb7 26. Nh2 Be6 27. Ndf3 Nc5+ 28. Kc2 Bb3+ 29. Kb1 Nd3 30. Be3 $1 {Aitken
White gives up the exchange, The play gets very tricky after 30.Re2} (30. Re2
Bc5 31. Be3 Rd8 {It can hold with best defence but we have no idea of the
times on the clock. (I suspect time trouble played a part.) Aitken tended to
shy away from complications if he could not see a clear path. Here he saw a
ghost or two and it was enough for him kill the tactics and play for a draw.
Geoff Chandler.}) 30... Nxe1 31. Ra7+ Kb8 32. Rxe7 Nxf3 33. Nxf3 f6 34. Rxg7
Rxg7 35. Nd2 Be6 36. Kc2 Kc7 37. h5 h6 38. Bxh6 Rh7 39. Be3 Rxh5 40. b3 Kc6 41.
b4 Bf7 42. Bc5 Bg6 43. Kd3 Rh1 44. Be7 f5 45. exf5 Bxf5+ 46. Ke2 Kd7 $1 {Aitken
} 47. Bg5 Rc1 48. Nf3 Rxc3 49. Nxe5+ Ke6 50. Bd2 Ra3 51. Nc6 Bg4+ 52. Kf1 Kd5
$1 {Aitken} 53. Ne7+ Ke4 54. Ng6 Ra1+ 55. Be1 Be6 $1 {Aitken} 56. Ke2 Ra2+ 57.
Kd1 Bb3+ 58. Kc1 Rc2+ 59. Kb1 Re2 60. Bc3 Rxf2 61. Be5 $2 {The blunder White
hoped to avoid and we knew would eventually come. Good game this one. G.C.} Bf7
62. Nf4 Rf1+ 63. Kc2 Kxe5 {Source: Aitken Book 10} 0-1

