1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. c4 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 e5 6. Nc2 Bc5 7. Be3 d6 8. Nc3
Nf6 9. Be2 Be6 10. O-O O-O 11. Kh1 Rc8 12. f3 Nh5 13. g3 Bxe3 14. Nxe3 Qg5 15.
Ng2 Qh6 16. Kg1 Nd4 17. b3 b5 18. f4 Nxe2+ 19. Qxe2 bxc4 20. f5 cxb3 21. fxe6
Rxc3 22. exf7+ Rxf7 23. Qxa6 Rxf1+ 24. Rxf1 Qe6 25. axb3 Nf6 26. b4 Rc2 27. Ne3
Ra2 28. Qd3 d5 29. Nxd5 Nxd5 30. Qxd5 Qxd5 31. exd5 Rb2 32. Re1 Rxb4 33. Rxe5
Rb5 34. Kf2 Kf7 35. g4 Kf6 36. Rf5+ Kg6 37. Ke3 Rb1 38. Kd4 Rb4+ 39. Ke5 Rxg4
40. Rf2 Rg1 41. Ke6 Re1+ 42. Kd7 Rd1 43. d6 {Source: Aitken Book 14. Note (JS):
this game score is a mystery. It was included in Aitken's scorebook with
three-character codes which are barely legible. He may not even have been one
of the players. One clue is the date, which he gives as Dec 1952 to June 1953.
So this might possibly be a correspondence game - possibly a game played in
the County & District Correspondence Championship (Ward-Higgs) competition
which ran from around Nov/Dec for abother six months or so. Geoff thinks the
three-character does read as given - I think they might be H5r and H5l (little
letter L ,or maybe H5e). Pairing numbers? EDIT (2 May 2021): further ideas
from the English Chess Forum - consultation/correspondence game between H5 
(left and right) where H may stand for 'Hut'. And Hugh Alexander was head of
H5 - the Russian crypt section - at GCHQ.} 1-0

