Rac1 Ne5 16. Qe2 Nxc4 17. Qxc4 Rfc8 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Rd8 20. Qc4 Bf6 21.
Bc5 Qc6 22. Qb4 Qd7 23. Bb6 Rac8 24. a5 Qe6 25. Rc4 Rxc4 26. Qxc4 Qxc4 27. bxc4
Rc8 28. c5 Bb2 {? Aitken} 29. Rb1 Ba3 {?? Aitken} 30. Rb3 Bc1 31. Kf1 Bd2 32.
Ke2 Bxa5 33. Bxa5 Rxc5 34. Bd8 Rc2+ 35. Kf3 b5 36. Bxe7 f5 37. g4 Kf7 38. Bb4
fxg4+ 39. hxg4 h5 40. g5 Rc4 41. Bc3 Ke6 42. Bf6 h4 43. Ra3 Rc6 44. Kg4 Rc2 45.
Rxa6+ Kd5 46. Kxh4 Rxf2 47. Bd4 Rh2+ 48. Kg3 Rh1 49. Rxg6 Rg1+ 50. Kf2 Rg4 51.
Rb6 Kc4 52. g6 {Note (JS): Aitken gives his opponent as "M Fischer" but, in
lieu of further information, I've given it as "F Fischer," a former champion
of Vienna, who was a member of West London CC from around 1943 to 1957. He
appeared in mid-1950s grading lists with a grade equivalent to 2300. EDIT:
Alan McGowan has searched further and found a Felix Fischer living in the
London area. A player of that name took part in the 1923 Schlechter Memorial
and 1934 Trebitsch Memorial tournaments, both of which were played in Vienna.
The London-resident Felix Fischer was born 27 February 1892 in Vienna, died 24
June 1967, at St Mary's Hospital, Paddington, London. He had previously been a
bank official (presumably in Vienna) but worked as a live-in butler in Surrey 
(alongside his wife Grete, also a domestic servant) at the time of the
outbreak of war when he was briefly interned. During the 1950s and 1960s he
lived in NW London. Source: Aitken Book 14} 1-0

