Nbd7 9. b4 e5 {An uncharacteristic slip by Aitken. He has missed the Knight
going d4 and f4 holding the e5 pawn. After this he never gets into the game.
White wraps it up very neatly.} 10. dxe5 Ng4 11. Nd4 Bg6 12. f4 Be7 13. Be2 h5
14. O-O O-O 15. Bd2 a5 16. h3 axb4 17. Nxd5 cxd5 18. hxg4 Bxc5 19. gxh5 Be4 20.
Rfc1 Rxa1 21. Rxa1 Bxd4 22. exd4 Rc8 23. Rc1 Rxc1+ 24. Bxc1 Nb6 25. Kf2 Nc4 26.
g4 Kf8 27. Kg3 Ke7 28. f5 Na5 29. Bg5+ f6 30. exf6+ gxf6 31. h6 Kf7 32. h7 Kg7
33. Bxf6+ Kxh7 34. Kf4 Nb3 35. g5 Nc1 36. g6+ Kg8 37. Bb5 Nd3+ 38. Kg5 Nxb2 39.
Bd7 {Source: Aitken Book 15. At the top of scoresheet Aitken adds that this
game ended his 21 game unbeaten run. (GC) Cheltenham won 3½-2½. The match
was reported in the Leicester Evening Mail, 15 February 1954. Other board
scores (Cheltenham names first): 2 DV Mardle 1-0 PN Wallis; 3 J Ansell 0-1 V
Russ; 4 J Neale ½-½ K Warren; 5 Dr IJ Good 1-0 PE Collier; 6 J Ball 1-0 R
Starosolsky. The Leicester board six lost on time in the closing minutes of
the match.} 1-0

