{"In the final round, with nothing at stake, I played in a more youthful and
carefree style. (Aitken, BCM)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4
exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 Nxe4 8. O-O Bxc3 9. d5 Ne5 10. Qe2 Nxf3+ 11. Qxf3 ({
In this position M.C.O. gives} 11. gxf3 Bf6 12. fxe4 d6 13. f4 O-O {and
considers the position is slightly in Black's favour. My line seems to be at
least as good and perhaps a trifle safer.}) 11... Bf6 {I consider Black is
correct to keep the bishop rather than the knight.} 12. Qxe4+ Qe7 13. Qc2 Be5 (
{If} 13... O-O 14. Bd3 {regains the pawn but in this position Black would be
well advised to throw a sop to Cerberus. Black's defiant decision to hold on
to his booty at all costs loses him the game.}) 14. Bd3 Qd6 15. f4 Bd4+ 16. Kh1
h5 17. Qe2+ Kf8 18. Qe4 c5 19. Re1 g6 20. f5 b6 21. fxg6 Bb7 22. gxf7 Bxd5 23.
Qe8+ Kg7 24. Bf4 $1 Qxf4 ({Black misses the point of this decoy sacrifice. The
best reply is} 24... Qf6 {but then} 25. f8=Q+ Rxf8 26. Qxh5 {wins.}) 25. f8=Q+
{Source: BCM, September 1956, p233} 1-0

