{"[Aitken] introduced some little-known theory to dispose of Bloodworth's pet
Alapin Defence to the Ruy Lopez in 24 moves." (CHESS, May 1957, p199 - vol.22
no.s 291-2)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Bb4 5. O-O Nge7 6. d4 exd4 7.
Nxd4 O-O $2 {? CHESS, May 1957, but analysis engines consider it best - JS} (
7... b5 $1 {Boleslavsky-Tartakower, Saltsjobaden 1948}) (7... d5 $2 8. exd5
Qxd5 $2 9. Nxc6 Qxd1 10. Nxb4+ Bd7 11. Rxd1 {1-0 Aitken-Hunter, British
Championship 1949 - Aitken's shortest ever win.}) 8. c4 Ng6 9. Nc2 Be7 10. Nc3
d6 11. Ne3 Bd7 12. Ncd5 Qc8 13. Bc2 Bh4 14. f4 Nce7 15. f5 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Ne5
17. f6 Ng6 18. e5 Qd8 19. Bxg6 fxg6 20. Qd4 g5 21. Ne7+ Kh8 22. fxg7+ Kxg7 23.
e6+ Kh6 24. Qxh4+ {Sources: CHESS, May 1957, p199; CHESS, Oct 1957, p30;
Burton Observer and Chronicle, 22 August 1968} 1-0

