{"The retiring champion, P. F. Copping, was unfortunate not to head Section A,
his solitary loss in round three against Good being due to an oversight whilst
pressing for a win. His defeat of Aitken in the last round was undoubtedly one
of the best played games of the event." (CHESS, May 1957, p199 - vol.22 No.s
291-2)} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 a6 4. e3 Bg4 5. Bxc4 e6 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3
Nc6 8. O-O Nf6 9. Rd1 Be7 10. Nc3 O-O 11. Qe2 Qc8 12. e4 e5 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14.
Bb3 Rd8 15. Bg5 h6 16. Bxf6 Rxd1+ 17. Rxd1 Bxf6 18. Nd5 Bd8 19. Qh5 Ng6 20.
Nxc7 Qxc7 21. Qxg6 Bf6 22. Qf5 Rf8 23. Rd7 Qc1+ 24. Kh2 Qxb2 25. g3 b6 26. Kg2
Qe5 27. Qf3 Qc3 28. Qh5 Qc6 29. Bxf7+ Kh8 30. Qd5 Qc5 31. Qxc5 bxc5 32. Bc4 a5
33. f4 Bd4 34. Rb7 g5 35. Kf3 gxf4 36. gxf4 Be5 37. Rf7 Rxf7 38. Bxf7 Kg7 39.
Bc4 Bd4 40. e5 Kg6 41. Ke4 h5 42. f5+ Kg5 43. f6 Kg6 44. Kf4 Bg1 45. Bd3+ Kf7
46. Kf5 {Source: CHESS, May 1957, p199. The 1957 West of England Championship
was divided into two sections, A and B. Aitken won section A with 4½/6, ahead
of 2-3 PF Copping, Dr IJ Good 4, 4-6 J Anderson, JE Jones, ME Ventham 3, etc.
Section B was won by Jeffrey Ansell, also with 4½/6 ahead of 2-3 BP Bonner,
WH Pratten 4, 4-5 RA Slade, DV Mardle 3½, etc. The two section winners later
met in a two-game decider.} 1-0

