[Event "National Club Ch Cheltenham-Leicester"]
[Site "Leicester"]
[Date "1958.01.25"]
[Round "2.1"]
[White "Aitken, James Macrae"]
[Black "Wallis, Philip Norman"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C02"]
[Annotator "Wood, BH"]
[PlyCount "58"]
[EventDate "1958.01.25"]
[EventType "team-match"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]
[SourceTitle "BritBase"]
[Source "John Saunders"]
[SourceDate "2020.10.25"]
[SourceVersion "1"]
[SourceVersionDate "2020.10.25"]
[SourceQuality "1"]
[WhiteTeam "Cheltenham"]
[BlackTeam "Leicester"]
[WhiteTeamCountry "ENG"]
[BlackTeamCountry "ENG"]

{When Leicester, the national club knock-out champions, met Cheltenham, former
champions, two of the strongest provincial clubs in the country clashed. To
win all the four games finished was a most impressive achievement on
Leicester's part. Dr. K. D. Warren, inciden­tally, travelled 600 miles from
Glasgow and back, especially to play. Results:— 1. PN Wallis 1-0 JM Aitken,
2. W Tabakiernik 1-0 J.Ansell, 3. PD Sanderson adj DV Mardle, 4. VJA Russ 1-0
NA McLeod, 5.Dr KD Warren 1-0 JC Cock, 6. R Starolsky adj J Neale. [final
score was 4½-1½] This extraordinary game was played at top board.} 1. e4 e6
2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 f6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 fxe5 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9.
dxe5 Bb4+ 10. Kf1 $2 ({Thinking to make some capital out of the loose
situa­tion of the checking bishop.} 10. Nc3 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qc7 {gives Black
chances against the isolated c-pawn. [But Houdini 4 Pro finds} 12. Qh5+ $1 Qf7
(12... g6 13. Bxg6+ hxg6 14. Qxh8 Qxc3+ 15. Ke2 $1 $18) 13. Bb5+ Kf8 14. Ba3+
Ne7 15. Qh4 $18 {almost certainly winning for White - JS]}) 10... Ne7 11. Qc2 (
{About here, most of Wallis’s team mates thought he was in an almost
desperate situation; but against} 11. Qa4+ Nc6 12. Bb5 {he had the neat
resource} O-O $1 13. Bxc6 Qb6 {threatening mate.}) 11... Bd7 12. Nc3 ({After} 
12. Bxh7 Rc8 13. Bg6+ Kf8 {White's queen cannot defend both bishops.}) 12...
Rc8 13. Be3 Nc6 14. Bxh7 Nxe5 15. f4 Qf6 16. Kg1 Qh6 17. Re1 Qxh7 {Now Black
wins a pawn. White has the drawing hope of bishops of opposite-coloured
squares. Neither of these factors compares in importance with the unfortunate
situation of White’s King and King's rook; the win for Black is, in fact,
not very difficult to find.} 18. Qxh7 Rxh7 19. fxe5 Bxc3 20. bxc3 Rxc3 21. Kf2
Rh5 22. Bd4 Rf5+ 23. Kg1 Rc2 24. a3 Rf4 25. Bxa7 d4 26. h4 Rg4 27. Rh2 Bc6 28.
Rd1 Bxg2 29. Rxd4 Be4+ {Source: Birmingham Daily Post, 4 February 1958 - BH
Wood column} 0-1

