1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Qe2 ({This move is just
as good as the more customary} 6. Re1) 6... b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 (8... d5 {
is also well playable here.}) 9. h3 ({This move, however, which is usual in
the lines with 6.Re1, is superfluous here. Correct is} 9. d4 Bg4 10. Rd1) 9...
Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Bg5 ({Again an unusual manoeuvre. The standard
plan is} 12. Rd1 {, followed by Nbd2, Nf1 and Ne3, aiaming at the eventual
control of the white squares in the centre.}) 12... h6 ({Not at once} 12... Nh5
{because of} 13. Nxe5) 13. Bh4 ({After this mistake, White is in real trouble.
He could still maintain a fair game by} 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. dxe5 dxe5 15. Rd1 {
followed by Nbd2, Nf1, Ne3.}) 13... Nh5 14. Bg3 ({If} 14. Bxe7 Nf4 15. Qd2 Qxe7
{and Black's powerfully placed knight is the basis for a strong king's side
attack.}) 14... Nxg3 15. fxg3 cxd4 16. cxd4 Qa7 {From now on, Black hammers
home his advantage in splendid style. His first task is to open the position
so that his two bishops can get to work.} 17. Kh2 exd4 18. e5 Nc4 ({Better than
} 18... dxe5 19. Qe4 g6 20. Nxe5 Bb7 21. Qg4 {, with dangerous counterplay.})
19. Qe4 g6 20. Nxd4 Bb7 21. Nc6 ({Now if} 21. Qd3 dxe5 {and White's forces are
in hopeless disorder}) ({or} 21. Qg4 Ne3) 21... Bxc6 22. Qxc6 Ne3 23. Rc1 Rfc8
{Your correspondent, sitting at the next board to Phillips, noticed his face
break into a beatific smile at this point as he saw the winning combination.}
24. Qe4 Rxc2 25. Qxc2 ({Equivalent to resignation, but if} 25. Rxc2 Nf1+ 26.
Kh1 Nxg3+ {wins the queen. The rest requires no comment - a fine attack
typical of Phillips at his best.}) 25... Nxc2 26. Rxc2 dxe5 27. Nc3 Qb7 28. Re1
Bb4 29. Rxe5 Bxc3 30. Rxc3 Rd8 {Source: The Guardian, 10 Apr 1958} 0-1

